  "query_preview": "/Through Tuesday/...\nIssued at 204 PM MST Tue Nov 3 2026\n\nWater vapor imagery shows relatively dry, zonal flow over Colorado \nbetween a departing trough over the Midwest and another trough over \nCalifornia. As a result, conditions will be dry today with breezy \nconditions across the plains. \n\nThe aforementioned California trough will move over Colorado \nWednesday afternoon. This is a weak, compact system that will \nprovide generally weak lift over a small portion of our forecast \narea. The main area that will likely (up to 80% chance) see \nprecipitation with this system is the I-70 corridor mountains, \nsouthern foothills, and Palmer Divide. Despite the forcing being \nweak, there will be decent instability in the higher terrain \nespecially in Park County considering the morning sunshine will\ncreate steep low level lapse rates. Showers and potentially a \ncouple of thunderstorms will develop in the afternoon in this \narea. By the evening, the showers will turn into more stratiform \nprecipitation and will spread out slightly. Model guidance is \nstarting to spread out a bit for this system as a few higher end \nsolutions have recently come in. Those seemingly indicate the \npotential this system could have to produce impactful snow \nespecially along Highway 285 in Park County and some areas in the \nfar southwestern foothills. The forecast currently calls for \nroughly 2-7\" of snow mainly in Park County and adjacent areas \nabove 7,500 feet. If there is an increasing trend in the \nprecipitation amounts for this system, a Winter Weather Advisory \nmay be needed due to poor travel conditions Wednesday night. \nHowever, there is uncertainty with the exact track of this system \nand if it were to go farther south, rain and snow amounts could be\nmuch lower. If the system tracks farther north, precipitation \ncould expand further northward in the mountains but the \nprecipitation across the plains will still be very limited. \n\nOn Thursday, a ridge aloft will move over Colorado which will dry\nconditions out. It will stay relatively cool with highs in the 50s\nacross the plains. \n\nA very weak shortwave trough will move just to the north of\nColorado late Friday into Saturday. There is very limited moisture\nwith this system and only a slight chance (20%) that any\nprecipitation occurs in the mountains. \n\nA broad ridge aloft will expand over the Intermountain West Sunday\nthrough early next week. This will lead to an extended period of\ndry, mild, and pleasant conditions.",
